{
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "concern",
      "claim": "The four witnesses (test_a_push_that_never_returns_is_refused_within_the_limit, test_a_timed_out_push_leaves_no_process_behind, test_an_interrupted_push_leaves_no_process_behind, test_a_push_asks_ssh_for_keepalive_only_where_the_host_names_no_ssh_command) all depend on wall-clock timing (0.1s heartbeat files, a 2-second patched PUSH_TIMEOUT_S, a SIGALRM fired at 2.5s, a 1-second flat-count check, a sub-5-second refusal bound) and on process-group/signal semantics, which is inherently more flake-prone under host load than ordinary unit tests. With max_attempts: 3 and budget_usd: 62, a cell that hits timing flakiness on a loaded host has little room to recover. An operator should decide whether this budget and attempt count are adequate given that the entire acceptance surface is timing-based.",
      "criterion": null,
      "file": null,
      "line": null,
      "fixes": null
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "claim": "The 'Why 120 seconds' note's claim that 'Keepalive gives up on a dead link after 60 seconds' is a loose paraphrase of ServerAliveInterval=15 / ServerAliveCountMax=4 (the actual worst case before ssh gives up can run a bit past 60s depending on when the first missed probe is counted from). The only load-bearing fact, PUSH_TIMEOUT_S (120) > 15*4 (60), holds regardless, so this costs nothing to leave as is.",
